Conky Clock Widget
==================
original by jpope (v1.1, 2010)
modernized for Conky 1.12.x on Bodhi/Moksha (2026)

An interactive conky clock. Click the LED on the clock face to view the
current month calendar; the calendar auto-returns to the clock after 10
seconds. Re-clicking the LED while the calendar is shown resets the timer.

What changed in the modern version
----------------------------------
* Config rewritten from legacy text syntax to the modern
  `conky.config = { ... }` / `conky.text = [[ ... ]]` Lua table syntax.
* The original jpope square faces are restored: the clock mode paints
  `images/bckgnd.png` (brushed-metal square with a round recessed dial)
  and the calendar mode paints `images/bckgnd_plain.png`. The analog
  clock is drawn into the dial well; the calendar grid sits on the plain
  plate. Both shoulders are composited onto an opaque backing in Lua so
  the desktop never bleeds through the metal.
* Look follows the "clockface1" design from the original preview: white
  hour/minute hands (no red second hand), line tick marks (no numerals),
  month above the dial, day below, and a blue LED.
* The widget is larger on screen: the conky window is 260x260 while the
  Lua design grid stays 175x175 and everything is scaled by
  G.s = min(window/175, window/175). Keep the conky min/max sizes and
  the btnclockwdgt SCALE constant in sync if you change this.
* The LED is drawn where the socket dot in the artwork sits (grid 154,155);
  btnclockwdgt pins its own button to the same spot via the real window
  geometry, re-asserting keep-above every second.
* The 10-second calendar timeout is handled inside Lua (uses the
  'clockswitch' state file timestamp), so the fragile `sleep 10s` shell
  command is gone.
* The LED/button no longer depends on theme images. btnclockwdgt is now
  a small fully-transparent GTK3 window that only catches the click. It
  pinpoints the LED by reading the conky window's real X geometry each
  second, so it stays aligned across start-up races and monitor changes.
* The Python clicker was updated from GTK2 to GTK3 (gi repository) and
  the old ADesk code was stripped out.

Files
-----
conkyclockwdgtrc     conky config (modern syntax; window 260x260)
conkyclock.lua       Cairo renderer: analog clock + calendar + LED
btnclockwdgt         GTK3 click overlay that flips the clock/calendar
conkyclock           start/stop/restart wrapper
clockswitch          state file ("0" = clock, "1" = calendar) - do not edit
images/bckgnd.png        square clock face (brushed metal + round well)
images/bckgnd_plain.png  square calendar plate

Setup
-----
1. Run `./conkyclock start` (or `~/.conky/clockwidget/conkyclock start`)
   to launch the widget together with its click button.
2. Use `./conkyclock stop` / `./conkyclock restart` as needed.
   Moksha autostart already calls this from ~/.conky/conky-startup.sh.

Positioning
-----------
* The widget sits top-right (gap_x 16, gap_y 50) - edit conkyclockwdgtrc.
* To move it, change alignment/gap_x/gap_y in the conky config, and the
  matching GAP_* constants in btnclockwdgt (the button auto-aligns to the
  drawn LED, but keep the fallback sane).
* The Lua design grid is 175x175; positions are scaled to the real window
  size automatically, so different DPI/monitors are fine.

Optional tuning in conkyclock.lua (USER OPTIONS block):
* CAL_TIMEOUT          seconds the calendar stays visible (default 10)
* colors (LEDON..LEDRING), clock face radius, LED position, fonts.

References (as in the original)
